window.CONCEPTS.CHAINS=[
{t:"Séance to light show: Spiritualism → Theosophy → Krishnamurti → Huxley → Leary",q:"Can a chain of people who met link a Vermont séance room to a 1960s New York light show?",
 n:["m_spirit","m_ts","c_krishna","c_huxley","c_leary","a_celeb"],
 v:"Yes. Every step is a documented meeting or working relationship: Blavatsky and Olcott meet at the Eddy brothers' séances (1874); Leadbeater and Besant take up the boy Krishnamurti at Adyar (1909); Krishnamurti and Huxley become lifelong friends in California (1938); Huxley sits on the board of Leary's Harvard Psilocybin Project (1960); Leary stages psychedelic 'celebrations' with projections by Jackie Cassen and Rudi Stern (1966). What it proves: an unbroken line of personal contact. What it does not prove: that one idea travelled unchanged. Krishnamurti rejected the Theosophical role made for him, and Huxley's drug mysticism was his own."},
{t:"The Tibetan Book of the Dead: Theosophy → Evans-Wentz → Leary → light shows → the Loft",q:"Is there a documented line from Theosophy to club culture?",
 n:["m_ts","c_tbd","c_leary","a_celeb","c_loft"],
 v:"The most direct one on the map, with one textual step. Evans-Wentz joined the Theosophical Society at Point Loma (1902) and published the Tibetan Book of the Dead (1927). Leary, Metzner and Alpert adapted it as The Psychedelic Experience (1964): a book used, not a meeting. Leary then staged its stages as light-and-sound celebrations (1966), and David Mancuso, who attended his lectures and private parties, built the Loft's three-bardo nights on it (1970)."},
{t:"Colour-light concert to light show: László → Fischinger → Harry Smith → Belson → San Francisco",q:"Is there a non-Theosophical line of live abstract projection to music that reaches the 1960s light shows?",
 n:["a_laszlo","a_raum","a_absfilm","a_smith","a_vortex","a_light"],
 v:"Yes, and mostly first-hand. Fischinger's films were added to László's colour-light concerts (1926). In San Francisco, Smith and Belson saw Fischinger's films together at Art in Cinema (1946); Smith wrote to him in 1947 and by 1950 was playing his hand-painted films live with bebop musicians, who improvised to the images. Smith's best friend Belson took live projection into the planetarium with Vortex (1957–59), the usual named precedent for the Bay Area light shows. Smith's own shows used painted film, not liquids; the liquid technique came separately, from Locks and Romero."},
{t:"Wilfred without Theosophy: Lumia → Belson's Vortex → San Francisco light shows",q:"Did Wilfred reach the 1960s light shows through artists rather than through Theosophy?",
 n:["a_wilfred","a_vortex","a_light"],
 v:"Through Belson, not Fischinger: no meeting between Fischinger and Wilfred is recorded. Belson saw Wilfred's Lumia in the late 1950s and changed his imagery (Moritz); Vortex at the Morrison Planetarium (1957–59) is the usual precedent named for the Bay Area light shows. The liquid technique itself came by a separate, first-hand route: Seymour Locks (1952) to his student Elias Romero to Bill Ham at the Avalon (1966)."},
{t:"Wilfred's Lumia: Theosophy → Clavilux → 1960s light shows",q:"Did the Theosophical light-art line reach the 1960s light shows?",
 n:["m_ts","a_wilfred","a_celeb","a_light"],
 v:"Partly first-hand. Wilfred built the Clavilux inside the Prometheans, a Theosophist group, with Claude Bragdon (1919–22): personal contact. Jackie Cassen, who made Leary's projections, and Joshua White of the Joshua Light Show are both described as directly influenced by Wilfred's lumia. Rudi Stern, Cassen's partner, called himself Wilfred's fan, remembered the lumia on show at MoMA (1964–81) and knew Wilfred's son (Stern, 1999). In February 1967 Cassen and Stern's 'Kinetic Projector #1' and Wilfred's lumia were in the same show, Howard Wise's Lights in Orbit. That is seeing the work and sharing a wall, not a recorded meeting, and neither dates when they first saw lumia. Cassen and Stern then ran New York light environments (1967), and Wise carried the same circle on into video art (1969)."},
{t:"Séance to Magic Theatre: Spiritualism → Jung → Hesse → Leary's Steppenwolf show",q:"Can a line of people link a séance to the light show that staged Steppenwolf's Magic Theatre?",
 n:["m_spirit","c_jung","c_hesse","c_leary","a_celeb"],
 v:"Yes, with one step through a book. As a student, Jung sat in on his cousin Helene Preiswerk's séances and made them his 1902 thesis. Hesse was analysed by Jung's pupil J. B. Lang (1916–17) and met Jung in 1917. Hesse died in 1962, so Leary and Metzner knew him only on the page, reading his novels as psychedelic guides (1963). In 1966 Leary's first Village Theater celebration, 'Death of the Mind', took the audience into Steppenwolf's Magic Theatre, with Metzner as Harry Haller and projections by Cassen and Stern. What it shows: the séance room turned into the unconscious and then into a light show. What it does not show: any Spiritualist belief surviving the trip."}
];
window.CONCEPTS.STRANDS=[
{t:"Thought-forms → abstraction → visual music",q:"Do Theosophical images of thought lead to abstract film and light shows?",
 n:["m_ts","i_vib","i_tf","i_music","o_chladni","a_kand","a_corra","a_klint","a_wilfred","a_raum","a_absfilm","a_vortex","a_mandala","a_light","a_vj","a_celeb"],
 v:"The best-documented strand. Kandinsky, Corra & Ginna and af Klint cite or echo Thought-Forms, and Wilfred worked inside a Theosophist group. Fischinger is only 'touched'. After 1950 the forms carry on while the doctrine fades: Belson and Whitney look to yoga and Jung, the light shows to Leary."},
{t:"Projection outside cinema",q:"Is the AV show that exists outside cinema rooted in Theosophy?",
 n:["g_lantern","p_schrop","g_phil","g_rob","g_pepper","a_castel","a_rim","g_fuller","a_scriabin","a_wilfred","a_laszlo","a_raum","g_attr","a_vortex","a_exp","a_struct","a_light","a_vj","a_celeb","a_video"],
 v:"Only partly. There are three roots. Showmanship: the magic lantern and ghost shows, centuries older than Theosophy. Colour music: begun as Enlightenment science, then given a spiritual mission by Theosophists (Scriabin, Wilfred). Modernist and materialist: the Bauhaus and the London Co-op, who used projectors to break illusion. And non-narrative projection was cinema's first state, so AV is less a break from cinema than a return to it."},
{t:"Before Fischinger",q:"What preceded Fischinger's Raumlichtkunst?",
 n:["o_kircher","o_newton","a_castel","a_rim","g_fuller","a_scriabin","a_corra","a_wilfred","a_greene","a_ruttmann","a_bauhaus","a_laszlo","a_raum"],
 v:"Direct sources: Ruttmann's Opus 1 with live music (1921), and László's Farblichtmusik (1925–26), where Fischinger's films were first projected. Parallel: Wilfred, Hallock-Greenewalt and the Bauhaus light plays. Older: Castel's and Rimington's colour organs, Scriabin's light part, Loie Fuller. Of all these, only Scriabin and Wilfred are clearly Theosophical."},
{t:"Spiritualism and stage magic",q:"When did avowed illusion split from the séance?",
 n:["g_scot","g_fairs","g_lantern","p_schrop","g_phil","g_rob","g_rh","m_spirit","m_uk","g_pepper","g_dav","g_mask","g_fox88","g_paul","g_mel","g_attr","g_houdini","g_holo"],
 v:"Magic shows long predate Spiritualism: Scot published conjurors' tricks in 1584 to debunk witchcraft, and Philidor turned fake séance ghosts into an avowed show in 1790. In Britain the split comes in 1864–65, when Maskelyne reproduced the Davenports' cabinet séance on stage. The line stays blurry: mediums used showbusiness methods, the Davenports never declared themselves Spiritualists, and magic theatres were where film arrived in 1896."},
{t:"Automatism",q:"From spirit guide to the unconscious",
 n:["m_spirit","i_auto","a_klint","o_psych","m_spr","c_surreal","c_cutup"],
 v:"Documented. The Surrealists took automatic writing from Spiritualism and psychical research and deliberately removed the spirits. Myers, Janet and Flournoy supplied the 'subliminal self' that replaced the spirit guide. Dada worked more with chance than with automatism."},
{t:"Chance as divination",q:"Is chance in art a form of divination?",
 n:["p_gebelin","p_levi","i_div","m_gd","o_iching","c_jung","c_suzuki","c_cage","a_gen","c_cutup","c_tarot"],
 v:"Mixed. The occult tarot is clearly an 18th–19th-century invention. Cage's chance comes from Zen and the I Ching, framed by Jung, and reached him through Suzuki, a Theosophical Society member. Generative computer art comes from information theory, with no occult element."},
{t:"Asian religion via Theosophy",q:"How much Eastern thought reached the counterculture through Theosophy?",
 n:["o_asia","m_ts","i_east","c_bukl","c_watts","c_suzuki","c_tbd","c_leary","c_loft","c_krishna","c_huxley","c_campbell","a_celeb","c_hesse"],
 v:"A lot of the channel, not the source. London's Buddhist Society began as a Theosophical lodge (Watts), Suzuki was a member, Evans-Wentz framed the Tibetan Book of the Dead, and Krishnamurti was raised by the TS. Vedanta (Vivekananda), Hesse's Siddhartha (from a missionary family) and later Asian teachers came by other routes."},
{t:"Steiner → Jung → Campbell?",q:"Does Theosophy flow through Steiner to Jung and Campbell?",
 n:["m_ts","m_anth","c_jung","m_bailey","c_eranos","c_krishna","c_campbell"],
 v:"Not through Steiner: no Steiner–Jung link is documented, and Jung dismissed both movements. The real channels are Mead's Gnostic scholarship (to Jung), Bailey's early Eranos (to Jung and Campbell), and Krishnamurti (to Campbell)."},
{t:"Vibration → vibes",q:"Does 'vibe' come from Theosophy?",
 n:["o_chladni","o_ether","p_mesmer","i_vib","m_newthought","c_vibes","a_cym","c_soundheal"],
 v:"Partly. The documented route runs from New Thought (Atkinson, 1906) through Peale to the 1960s. Theosophy held the same idea in parallel, and both drew on popular physics."},
{t:"New Thought and Thought-Forms",q:"Which came first: thoughts as things, or auras and thought-forms?",
 n:["p_mesmer","p_reich","p_swed","p_davis","p_quimby","p_babbitt","m_newthought","i_attract","m_ts","o_baraduc","i_vib","i_tf","i_aura","i_ntaura","c_vibes","c_newage"],
 v:"Siblings from mesmerism, not parent and child. New Thought had 'thoughts are things' and thought-currents first (Mulford, 1886–89), but no pictures. The seen, coloured aura is older than both (odic light, Babbitt 1878); Theosophy turned it into a colour code with emotional meanings in 1896–1903 and drew thought-forms. New Thought then borrowed that visual system wholesale: Atkinson's aura and thought-form books of 1903 and 1912. For art, the Theosophical side matters, because it came with images."},
{t:"New Age ideas from Theosophy",q:"Which New Age ideas come directly from Theosophy and the occult?",
 n:["m_ts","m_bailey","i_aura","i_chakra","i_masters","i_planes","c_newage","c_chakra","c_channel","c_tarot","i_div"],
 v:"The clearest direct inheritances are the term 'New Age' (Bailey), the coloured aura and the Western chakra system (Leadbeater), Masters as the source of channelled teaching, the astral body, and the divinatory tarot (Golden Dawn). Western popular karma and reincarnation also came largely through Theosophy (from memory)."},
{t:"Science as spur (1770s–1910s)",q:"Which scientific innovations fed Spiritualism, Theosophy and their siblings?",
 n:["o_magnet","p_mesmer","o_telegraph","m_spirit","o_photo","o_baraduc","o_darwin","i_evol","m_ts","o_crookes","o_zollner","i_planes","o_keely","i_vib","o_edison","o_wattsh","i_music","o_ether","o_xray","i_occchem","i_tf","a_psych","a_kand"],
 v:"Each new invisible force became a model for spirit: Newtonian forces and magnets for Mesmer's fluid, the telegraph for spirit raps, photography for spirit 'extras' and thought plates, the fourth dimension for Zöllner's spirits, ether waves for telepathy, X-rays and the electron for clairvoyant atoms and dissolving matter. Theosophy wrote this into its 1878 objects (study 'magnetism, electricity and all other forms of force') and courted scientists: Crookes joined, Edison signed. Evolution is the exception: an opponent, recast rather than borrowed. Much of this is shared vocabulary (parallel), not transmission; the documented links are memberships, citations (Keely, Watts Hughes, Crookes's diagram) and named experiments."},
{t:"Science as proof: the counterculture (1920s–70s)",q:"Does the pattern repeat after Theosophy's heyday?",
 n:["o_ether","o_theremin","a_absfilm","o_eeg","o_grey","a_dream","o_psyched","c_huxley","c_leary","a_mandala","a_light","i_aura","o_kilner","o_kirlian","c_chakra","c_krishna","o_quantum","c_newage","a_celeb"],
 v:"Yes, with new instruments. Radio oscillators (Theremin, with Bute), EEG and alpha biofeedback, mescaline and LSD research, Kirlian photography and quantum physics each gave AV artists tools and the New Age 'proof'. The Theosophical thread is thinner: the clearest lines are the aura (Leadbeater's colours read into Kilner's screens and Kirlian coronas) and Krishnamurti's dialogues with Bohm. Most of it is occulture: diffusion with the labels lost."},
{t:"Magic as method: Golden Dawn to TOPY",q:"Where does the Western magical tradition, rather than Theosophy, shape AV and the underground?",
 n:["p_levi","m_gd","a_pcs","i_div","a_colquhoun","m_thelema","a_eleusis","i_sigil","a_anger","a_jarman","c_cutup","a_indust","c_chaos","c_topy","c_occulture","a_tech","a_vj"],
 v:"Smaller and later than the Theosophical line, but the main occult route into underground film, industrial music and acid house. The Golden Dawn had about a hundred members in the mid-1890s and kept its rituals secret until Crowley (1909–13) and Regardie (1937–40) printed them, so its influence arrived decades late. What it handed on was method more than imagery: ritual as performance (Eleusis, Anger), sigils and will (Spare, chaos magic, TOPY), tarot and correspondences. Theosophy gave modernism pictures of the invisible; magic gave the underground procedures."},
{t:"Two occult film lines: abstraction vs ritual",q:"Does Theosophy or magic shape occult film and AV more?",
 n:["m_ts","i_tf","a_kand","a_raum","a_absfilm","a_vortex","a_mandala","a_exp","m_thelema","a_eleusis","a_anger","a_jarman","a_indust","c_topy","a_tech"],
 v:"They shape different kinds of work. Theosophy (with Eastern sources) runs through abstract, non-figurative visual music: colour, form, cosmos, from Kandinsky and Fischinger to Belson and the Whitneys. Magic runs through figurative, costumed, ritual cinema and live performance: Eleusis (1910), Anger, Cameron and Harrington, Jarman, then industrial AV and TOPY. One offers images of the invisible to look at; the other offers rites to perform, and so suits time-based, performed work. The magick line is also markedly queer and transgressive, which kept it underground and out of art history until the 2000s."}
];
